        "id": "ch1_3_7-2",
        "text": "Let\u2019s suppose you inserted a USB key; then mounting a directory is necessary\nto access your USB drive contents. This is applicable if you disabled the auto-\nmount feature in your settings (which is on by default in the Kali 2020.1 release).\n24\nChapter 1 I Mastering the Terminal Window\nTo mount a USB drive, follow these steps:\n1.I Display the disk list using the lsblk command.\n2. Create a new directory to be mounted (this is where you will access the\nUSB stick drive).\n3.I Mount the USB drive using the mount command.\nNow, to eject the USB drive, use the umount command to unmount the directory:\nroot@kali-laptop-hp:~# umount /mnt/usb\nManaging Text Files in Kali Linux\nKnowing how to handle files in Kali Linux is something that you\u2019ll often encounter\nduring your engagements. In this section, you will learn about the most common\ncommands that you can use to get the job done.\nThere are many ways to display a text file quickly on the terminal window.",
